\DOC LOCAL_SUB_P256K1_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SUB_P256K1_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes an inlined modular-subtraction subroutine, advancing the
goal's machine state from one symbolic state to the next and adding an
assumption characterising the stored difference modulo the prime.

\DESCRIBE
Takes an {int}, the offset/index locating the code block to be executed, and a
{string list} of three operand names — the result variable followed by the two
input variables — which fix the new names occurring in the added assumption and
in the memory-read assumptions. The goal is an {eventually} statement about a
symbolic state, with assumptions describing the state at that point. One
subgoal results, in which the state variable is replaced by its successor both
in the conclusion and in the state-describing assumptions: all
{read}/{bytes_loaded}/{MAYCHANGE} assumptions are discharged and re-added for
the new state, with an updated program-counter assumption and a new assumption
recording the memory location now holding the result value. A further
implication assumption is added, of the form "{t < m} and {u < m} imply the
integer value of the result equals {(&t - &u) rem &m}", where {t} and {u} are
the values of the two input operands. Pre-existing non-state assumptions
(definitional equations and earlier such implications) are kept, sometimes in a
different order.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces the behaviour is this same single-subgoal state advance,
always on goals whose assumptions already describe a full symbolic machine
state.

\ENDDOC
